# modules/image/checks-config.rules - the container-image module's own
# per-owner registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's `checks-<name>.rules` row,
# docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29) for the module's three CONFIG-BLOB checks:
# `IMAGE-CFG-RUNS_AS_ROOT-01` (IMG-06); `IMAGE-CFG-EXPOSED_PORTS-01` and
# `IMAGE-CFG-MUTABLE_BASE_REF-01` (IMG-10) - root user, exposed ports, and a
# mutable base tag.
#
# `script: config.sh` for all three - modules/image/config.sh is the owning
# driver (`image_check_root_user`/`image_check_exposed_ports`/
# `image_check_mutable_base_ref`), a NEW file rather than an addition to
# distro/apk.sh or engine.sh: every one of these checks is DISTRO-AGNOSTIC
# (it reads the image's own CONFIG blob, not a Dockerfile
# and not any apk/dpkg-specific data), so they belong to neither this
# module's package-manager-specific drivers nor a future rpm one.
#
# `tags: passive` / `tags: quick` - each reads one already-extracted local
# config blob, no network call, no mutation - the identical reasoning
# modules/image/checks-apk.rules and checks-advisories.rules already give
# for their own tags.
#
# `coverage-scope: image-id` - the only legal value for the `IMAGE` module
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's frozen table).
#
# `IMAGE-CFG-RUNS_AS_ROOT-01`'s `severity: medium` is deliberately
# one step below `IAC-DOCKER-ROOT_USER-01`'s `high`: this check
# confirms the EFFECTIVE runtime user from the built artifact, but (unlike
# source review) it cannot see whether a later `docker run --user` override
# at deploy time already corrects it, so it is reported at a slightly more
# conservative severity than its source-side counterpart.
#
# `IMAGE-CFG-EXPOSED_PORTS-01`'s `severity: info` / `cwe: none` /
# `owasp: none` - the identical convention checks-coverage.rules already
# uses for a purely descriptive, non-defect finding: this check is a
# built-artifact attack-surface INVENTORY - a built-artifact counterpart to
# reviewing EXPOSE lines - not an assertion
# that any port is itself a vulnerability - an EXPOSE/ExposedPorts entry
# documents intent and does not by itself open anything (see the check's
# own remediation text in modules/image/config.sh).
#
# `IMAGE-CFG-MUTABLE_BASE_REF-01`'s `severity: low` / `cwe: CWE-829` /
# `owasp: A08:2021` - the identical mapping `modules/iac/dockerfile.rules`'
# `IAC-DOCKER-UNPINNED_DIGEST-01` already uses (this check fires on ANY
# non-digest-pinned base reference, the broader of that file's two related
# checks' conditions, not only a literal `:latest` tag), restated under this
# module's own 2021 OWASP-year convention (modules/image/checks-apk.rules,
# checks-dpkg.rules) rather than dockerfile.rules' 2025 one.

id: IMAGE-CFG-RUNS_AS_ROOT-01
title: Image config declares no non-root USER - the effective runtime user is root
script: config.sh
severity: medium
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-250
owasp: A04:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: Add (or fix) a non-root USER in the Dockerfile that built this
  image's final stage, or in whichever base image sets it, then rebuild and
  re-scan. A base-image bump alone can change the effective user without
  any Dockerfile line changing, so re-check this after any FROM tag/digest
  bump too. This is the built-artifact counterpart to
  IAC-DOCKER-ROOT_USER-01, which reads only one Dockerfile's own USER
  instruction and cannot see the effective user across a base image or a
  multi-stage build.
references: CWE-250
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A04_2021-Insecure_Design/

id: IMAGE-CFG-EXPOSED_PORTS-01
title: Image config declares one or more exposed network ports
script: config.sh
severity: info
confidence: high
cwe: none
owasp: none
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: Review whether every port listed in this finding's evidence
  genuinely needs to be reachable from this container's default network.
  An ExposedPorts entry (the built-artifact record of a Dockerfile's own
  EXPOSE instruction) documents intent but does not by itself open
  anything - the actual exposure is decided by how the container is run
  (-p/--publish, a Kubernetes Service, ...) - so treat this as an
  attack-surface inventory to cross-check against that runtime
  configuration, not as a vulnerability on its own.

id: IMAGE-CFG-MUTABLE_BASE_REF-01
title: Image's recorded base image reference is a mutable tag, not a content digest
script: config.sh
severity: low
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-829
owasp: A08:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: Rebuild from the base image's own @sha256:<digest> reference
  rather than a mutable tag (docker pull <ref> && docker inspect --format
  '{{index .RepoDigests 0}}' <ref>, or read the digest off the registry),
  so a later repoint of the tag cannot silently change what this image was
  built from on the next rebuild. This is the built-artifact counterpart
  to IAC-DOCKER-UNPINNED_DIGEST-01/IAC-DOCKER-LATEST_TAG-01 - fixing the
  Dockerfile's own FROM line is what prevents this on the NEXT build; this
  finding is about the image already built. This check applies only when
  the image's own config records a base image reference at all
  (org.opencontainers.image.base.name, an opt-in buildkit provenance
  annotation) - most built images do not carry this label, which this
  module records as a declared coverage limitation rather than a guess.
references: CWE-829
